I suspect like many people in this room  I fell in love with the beats at college  I can remember going to the campus  bookstore and seeking out the poetry  aisle in the Ginsberg collections which  felt to me like a lifeline I mean I was  an undergraduate a closeted kid from New  Jersey who wanted to be a creative  writer and I mean Allen Ginsberg was  meant the world to me I mean he was just  he's openness and his honesty just it  felt to me like a transmission from the  person I wanted to be when you start to  get into the beats you can only you  can't just do a little bit and there are  a lot of biographies out there and this  murder appears as a footnote in many of  them this murder was front-page news in  the New York Times in 1944 so knowing  that and knowing that these three  amazing most beloved writers of the 20th  century were involved in this murder it  just seemed to me kind of the only  mystery was why it hadn't been tackled  before dramatically so that's when I  went to John who is my college roommate  and I suggested that I told him I wanted  to write it as a nonfiction book and  John do you want to tell them about the  Jedi mind trick that you pulled on my  brain Austin came to me with the idea of  doing this as a piece potentially a  fiction ten years ago I had just  finished film school done a couple of  shorts and Austin was beginning to get  some renown as a short story writer and  a playwright and as he's telling me the  story that he wants to tell I of course  thing start seeing the movie in the back  of my head and I decided to convince him  that I don't know if it would work as a  short story but as a movie it would be  amazing but what was really poignant  about this is then I asked him if we  could collaborate and work on it  together and you know we've been friends  since we were 18 years old and we've  shared you know all of our work up until  that point  Austin's actually the first short film I  ever made on super 8 before I really  knew what I was doing I showed it to  Austin and he said to me John it doesn't  stink and it's much better than I  thought it was going to be but that was  exactly the encouragement I needed at  that time to keep going and in terms of  do we do it as a crime drama I mean we  knew we had an event and we had this  event you know the story that nobody  really knew but yet was the thing that  caused Allen Ginsberg Jack Kerouac and  William  to stop talking about art and what they  wanted to do to change the world as  college students and to really put pen  to page and become writers and start  their revolution who we realized you  know the character with the greatest arc  over the course of the story was Allen  Ginsberg because he starts off at the  top of the movie a dutiful son who takes  care of his emotionally ill mother whose  father is a struggling poet and he knows  he has a voice inside and he's scared  you doesn't want to disappoint his  father and say maybe I want to do this  and do this in my own way and then of  course he meets this beautiful and  charismatic young man who is somewhat  also emotionally ill are troubled and  again in a way becomes his caretaker but  by the end of the story you know it's a  classic story I think we've all been  through this of learning to take care of  yourself and find your own voice and  that idea a movie about someone our  favorite you know one of our favorite  authors finding their own voice Daniel  let me let's follow up with that and  tell us what resonated for you when you  read this script I mean you're you're  from England and you're playing one of  America you know American literature's  and poetry's American icons now in  Ginsberg I think one of the things that  I love about it it's just and the thing  that jumps out at you about the script  is the fact that it the story is  constantly being moved along but it's  never becomes just expositional it's  always moved along through exchanges  based on character and relationships and  that's always the mark of I think very  good writing Ginsburg's nationality much  like his sexuality or his religion don't  really weren't things that I  particularly focused on and intimidated  by just because like I knew that he's  American so I'll do both find the accent  I'll work on the accident I'll you know  get that and and and but really the  things excited me about the character  was that when you read his Diaries as a  teenager he was just a really really was  unusual and interesting and and and and  a character you know worth putting on  film regardless of his later fame you  know he was he's interesting enough of  his you know standing alone at this  point and you know it's that really it's  the split then John it's something John  and I have talked about a lot with a lot  of creative people operate on this  system kind of veering between poles of  anxiety and ambition and and you know  that's what Allen was as a teenager he  was both desperately insecure on some  level and then very shy socially but  also incredibly bright it had a men  confidence in his in his intellect and I  think that it's the sort of the gap  between those two things is where really  every interesting character kind of lies  and and trying to you know reconcile  those two things John worked with me in  a way that I've never been worked with  before and and put time in with me that  you know due to the nature of the jobs  that I've done hasn't been necessarily  time and and and John for the year that  I was doing how to succeed before we did  kill your darlings John and I would meet  at least sort of once a month and this  started going through the script going  through scenes while yes in what I was  doing how to take yeah so we did some  Sunday and then we just do like yeah we  just go through you know work on  accident physicality and because that's  the other thing about and he's wasn't  athletic at all I track but he had a  really interesting physicality and he  was 